{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>A land in which you shall not eat bread in poverty.</b> A land that is full of every good thing, like the garden of HaShem, like the land of Egypt, yet lacks certain things found in other places — its inhabitants eat bread in poverty. Therefore Scripture praises the Holy Land, in which there is no lack of anything in the world: there is no food for man found in any other land that is not found in this land, and there are things found in this land that are not found in other lands; this is the meaning of ״you shall not lack anything in it״." "<b>And it is possible</b> that the phrase ״a land in which you shall not eat bread in poverty״ further alludes to the matter of Torah and wisdom — for Torah is compared to bread, as it is said (Proverbs 9:5): ״לכו לחמו בלחמי״. And since Torah and wisdom cannot be attained by man in their perfection except with the proper preparations required for them, and besides the preparations one needs the air of the land to be pure and good, for it is of great benefit for learning, and the good air of the land is among the best preparations — therefore it says that the wise men of the land shall not eat its bread of Torah in poverty, for their heart will be as open as the entrance of a hall for the good preparations, and as broad as the sea for the good air. And as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Genesis 2:11–12): ״וזהב הארץ ההיא טוב״ — there is no Torah like the Torah of the land of Israel, and there is no wisdom like the wisdom of the land of Israel; and they said: the air of the land of Israel makes one wise. This is not so with the wise men outside the land, who lack the preparations and the good air, and who are surely eaters of the bread of sorrow, eaters of the bread of Torah in poverty. And this is what it says in praise of the land: ״a land in which you shall not eat bread in poverty״ — since their Torah is whole, that is the enduring and true wealth with which they are blessed, as it says (Proverbs 10:22): ״ברכת ה' היא תעשיר״; and therefore it says: ״you shall not lack anything in it,״ for the blessing is in everything.\n\nAnd the sense of ״whose stones are iron״ is: in a place where you would expect to find stones, there you will find iron; and likewise in the mountains, there you will find copper. This is ״you shall not lack anything in it״ according to its plain sense, for the absence of iron and copper is a great deficiency in a land, since they are the implements most commonly used. But silver and gold are not a great deficiency in a land; therefore Scripture praised the land for what is essential for use — namely iron and copper, whose absence would be a great deficiency — and did not praise it for silver and gold. And so Moses mentioned this in the blessing of Asher (Deuteronomy 33:25): ״ברזל ונחשת מנעלך״.\n\nAnd the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ארץ אשר אבניה ברזל״ — do not read אבניה (its stones) but בוניה (its builders); these are the disciples of the wise. The Sages of blessed memory intended by this the matter of faith, and they took this verse as an asmakhta."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {1 "R. Bahya's citation reads '(משלי ט׳:ה׳)' for לכו לחמו בלחמי, which is Proverbs 9:5 — correctly cited. His second quotation, 'ברכת ה' היא תעשיר,' is Proverbs 10:22; he cites it without a reference, so none added."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:02.825800979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}